  <data name="TimeSync_HelpBody" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Why it matters
· Website certificates, sign-ins, captchas, and ticket bookings all rely on an accurate system clock; if it drifts too far, things fail even when the network is fine.
· Windows ships with time.windows.com as its default time source, which can time out or respond slowly on some networks — a common cause of "some websites just feel off". Switching to a nearby time source fixes it.

Three steps
1. Check the status card above: is the time service running, what is the current source, and when did it last sync?
2. Click "Server Speed Test" in the top-right corner to see which servers respond and their latency; results appear right on the cards.
3. Pick one (green or amber badges both work; green is faster) → "Apply and Sync Now".

Changed your mind?
· Click "Restore System Default": domain PCs go back to the domain hierarchy, regular PCs go back to time.windows.com.

Going deeper
· Advanced settings let you pin a sync interval (system-adaptive by default), toggle the SSL time seed, and reset the time service;
· "Copy Diagnostic Report" collects service status, time source, NTP configuration, and probe results into text you can paste for help.

Permissions
· Changing the time source and starting/stopping the time service require administrator rights; the unpackaged build requests elevation at startup, and you can also use "Restart as Administrator".
· Everything goes through the built-in w32tm command (see Microsoft Learn: Windows Time service tools and settings) — no drivers, no background process.</value>
  </data>
