  <data name="TimeSync_Why1.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· Website certificates have validity periods; if the clock is too far off, browsers treat them as invalid ("Not secure / Your connection is not private"). Sign-in checks, captchas, and ticket bookings also fail when timestamps do not line up.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeSync_Why2.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· Windows defaults to time.windows.com, which occasionally times out or responds slowly on some networks — a common reason "some websites feel off" even though the network is fine. Switching to a nearby time source fixes it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeSync_Why3.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· This tool drives the built-in w32tm command (the official Windows Time service CLI). No drivers, no background process; switching the time source simply writes the NTP server list and restarts the time service, and "Restore system default" is always available.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeSync_Why4.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· Changing the time source and starting/stopping the service require administrator rights: the unpackaged build requests elevation automatically at startup, or use the "Restart as Administrator" button above.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeSync_Why5.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>· Official reference: Microsoft Learn, "Windows Time service tools and settings" — w32tm /config /manualpeerlist:&lt;servers> /syncfromflags:manual /update, w32tm /resync /rediscover, w32tm /query /status and /query /source.</value>
  </data>
